family: net-snmp
what: poll
transport: v2c
address: 192.0.2.10
verification: tested
reference: net-snmp manual pages: snmpd.conf(5) (agentaddress, rocommunity, rocommunity6, createUser, rouser)
does: Lets Woodshed poll this machine's snmpd (SNMPv2c, read-only, from Woodshed's address only).
check: Devices → Add polling → Test poll answers with this machine's name and interfaces; after the first cycle the device page shows its graphs.
placeholders: <COMMUNITY>
addresses: 192.0.2.10 2001:db8::10 logs-01.example.net logs-01
notes:
- Then add this machine in Woodshed: Devices → Add polling, its address, port 161, SNMPv2c and the same community; Test poll shows what answers.
- The source address limits the community to polls from 192.0.2.10. If Woodshed reaches this machine through NAT, use the address the polls arrive from.
- Edit the agentaddress line rather than adding a second one: snmpd joins every agentaddress line it reads, so the distribution's usual `agentaddress 127.0.0.1,[::1]` would stay, and two entries for port 161 can stop snmpd from starting. The new line listens on every address, IPv4 and IPv6; to keep loopback-only addresses too, list them on the same line, comma-separated.
- Keep snmpd.conf readable by root only: it holds the community.
---- part file "/etc/snmp/snmpd.conf": Replace the existing agentaddress line with this one (add it if there's none)
agentaddress udp:161,udp6:161
---- part file "/etc/snmp/snmpd.conf": Then add these lines to the same file, and restart snmpd (sudo systemctl restart snmpd)
# Woodshed: answer polls (SNMPv2c, read-only).
rocommunity <COMMUNITY> 192.0.2.10
==== text
# /etc/snmp/snmpd.conf: Replace the existing agentaddress line with this one (add it if there's none)
agentaddress udp:161,udp6:161

# /etc/snmp/snmpd.conf: Then add these lines to the same file, and restart snmpd (sudo systemctl restart snmpd)
# Woodshed: answer polls (SNMPv2c, read-only).
rocommunity <COMMUNITY> 192.0.2.10
